Good:
  Inference only produces code that would be accepted if written by hand: every inferred
  capability, type and annotation can be written in source with the same meaning.
  A:{ read .b: mutH B -> {}; ... }   is inferred as   A:{ read .b: mutH B -> mut B; ... }
Bad:
  A:{ read .b: mutH B -> {}; ... }   is inferred as   A:{ read .b: mutH B -> mutH B; ... }
  (the parser rejects mutH and readH in front of a literal or a type expression, so a
  hygienic capability on an inferred literal or type expression is a bug; the same holds for
  any other inferred output that could not be written)
Good:
  base.BaseContainer, like base.Sealed, is a flag type: it is only implemented, never used
  as a type. Code using it as a type may be rejected.
  MyId:base.BaseId[base.BaseContainer[A],base.BaseContainer[B]]{ #(x)->x.as{::} }   is rejected
Good:
  An overriding method refines only nominal types: the reference capability of its result
  and of each parameter stays as in the overridden method (X and rc X count as the same
  when the bound of X is exactly {rc}).
  Sup:{ mut .h: mut P }  Sub:Sup{ mut .h: iso P }   is rejected
Good:
  A generic type can be implemented twice with type arguments differing in capabilities;
  overriding decides whether its methods can be reconciled.
  Foo[T:*]:{}  A:Foo[imm Bar],Foo[mut Beer]{}   is accepted
  Foo[T:*]:{ .get: T }  A:Foo[imm Bar],Foo[mut Beer]{}   is rejected, no .get can keep both capabilities
Good:
  Every instance of a base.CaptureFree type has an empty ROG: the type and all its subtypes
  capture nothing, so a mut and an imm reference to the same instance cannot observe any
  difference. The self name of a CaptureFree declaration inside a method keeps the
  capability of the declaration (imm by default) in every method, even when the
  declaration is created by name as mut.
  A0:{.m:A->A:base.CaptureFree{'self read .get:imm A->self}}
  B:{.m:mut A->mut A; .alias(a:mut A):imm A->a.get}   is accepted
Good:
  The type system does not track objects that are guaranteed imm, or imm in practice, without
  being typed imm (capture free objects, objects with no mutable state reachable, ...): sound
  programs relying on that may be rejected, also where a similar case is accepted. The
  standard library offers methods to turn such values into imm; those are used instead of
  extending the type system.
  A:base.CaptureFree{ read .me: imm A -> this }   is rejected
